// Storms at the 30 busiest US departure airports, May-July 2026, joined to their departures. Built by
// storms/build_storms.sh (IEM ASOS/METAR observations + NOAA Storm Events + the BTS flights files) and loaded into
// MotherDuck ltools.flights.storm_*. Answers: when storms hit, which airports and airlines cancel or delay more
// than the storm itself justifies?

#" STORM SEVERITY per airport and day (2,760 rows: 30 airports x 92 days, May-July 2026), from the airport's own weather observations, with NOAA storm reports.
#(agent) STORM SEVERITY per airport and day (2,760 rows: 30 airports x 92 days, May-July 2026), from the airport's own weather observations (IEM ASOS/METAR archive, operating hours 6am-10pm local). severity_band: "0 None", "1 Minor" (no thunder; low cloud under 1,000 ft, visibility under 3 mi or gusts 30 kt+), "2 Moderate" (thunder 1-3 hours), "3 Severe" (heavy thunder, or thunder 4+ hours), "4 Extreme" (heavy thunder AND thunder 4+ hours). Storm day = Severe or Extreme. ts_hours = hours with a thunderstorm reported at the airport; heavy_ts_hours = hours with heavy thunder (+TS); peak_gust_kt = highest gust or peak wind; intensity_cell = thunder hours x heavy thunder hours x peak gust bin, used to match "the same storm" across airports. storm_reports = NOAA Storm Events reports (thunderstorm wind, hail, tornado, flash flood, lightning, heavy rain) within 40 km that day, May and June only (July not yet published by NOAA; null in July), with the strongest reported wind and largest hail. Sources: IEM ASOS archive (Iowa State University, redistributing National Weather Service observations, free); NOAA NCEI Storm Events Database (public domain).
source: storm_days is motherduck.sql("""
  SELECT * FROM ltools.flights.storm_days
""") extend {
  dimension:
    #" True for Severe and Extreme days (heavy thunder, or thunder 4+ hours).
    is_storm_day is severity_band = '3 Severe' or severity_band = '4 Extreme'
    #" Month name, e.g. "June 2026".
    month_label is concat(
      pick 'May' when month(flight_date) = 5 pick 'June' when month(flight_date) = 6 else 'July', ' 2026')
  measure:
    #" Airport-days.
    airport_days is count()
    #" Severe or Extreme days.
    storm_day_count is count() { where: is_storm_day }
    #" Average hours with thunder at the airport.
    avg_thunder_hours is ts_hours.avg()
    #" Average hours with heavy thunder.
    avg_heavy_thunder_hours is heavy_ts_hours.avg()
    #" Average highest gust (knots).
    avg_peak_gust_kt is peak_gust_kt.avg()
    #" Average NOAA storm reports within 40 km per day (May-June days only).
    reports_per_day is storm_reports.avg()
    #" Strongest thunderstorm wind reported within 40 km (knots, May-June).
    max_reported_wind_kt is report_max_wind_kt.max()

  #" PLAYBOOK "Are storms rougher at some airports?" Storm days per airport and how rough they were: thunder hours,
  #" heavy-thunder hours, peak gust, and NOAA severe-weather reports within 40 km (May-June). Chicago storm days
  #" averaged about 24-27 reports nearby (winds to 74 kt); Atlanta, Tampa and Fort Lauderdale storm days had none.
  #" Caveat: report counts also depend on how many spotters live nearby (dense metros report more).
  view: storm_intensity_by_airport is {
    where: is_storm_day
    group_by: origin
    aggregate:
      storm_day_count
      avg_thunder_hours
      avg_heavy_thunder_hours
      avg_peak_gust_kt
      reports_per_day
      max_reported_wind_kt
    order_by: reports_per_day desc
  }
}

#" Hourly weather at the 30 airports (66,059 rows), local hour 0-23: hour_level 0 none, 1 minor, 2 moderate (thunder, or gusts 45 kt+, visibility under 1 mi, cloud under 500 ft, 0.5 in rain).
#(agent) Hourly weather at the 30 airports (66,059 rows), local hour 0-23: hour_level 0 none, 1 minor, 2 moderate (thunder, or gusts 45 kt+, visibility under 1 mi, cloud under 500 ft, 0.5 in rain), 3 severe (heavy thunder, hail, or thunder with 35 kt+ gusts or visibility under 1 mi).
#(agent) For exploring which hours a storm hit.
source: storm_hours is motherduck.sql("""
  SELECT * FROM ltools.flights.storm_hours
""") extend {
  measure:
    hour_count is count()
    thunder_hours is count() { where: ts }
}

#" Bootstrap 95% intervals (400 resamples of airport-days, storm days only) for the storm-day ratios, by kind and subject.
#(agent) Bootstrap 95% intervals (400 resamples of airport-days, storm days only) for the storm-day ratios: kind = "airport cancellations" (subject = airport code), "airline cancellations" / "airline late departures" (subject = BTS carrier code), "regional vs mainline, same route-day" (subject = the comparison). lo/hi bound the observed / expected ratio (or the regional / mainline cancel-rate ratio).
source: storm_ci is motherduck.sql("""
  SELECT * FROM ltools.flights.storm_ci
""")

#" START HERE for storm questions (fast: 167,966 pre-summed rows).
#(agent) Departures from the 30 busiest US airports, May- July 2026 (1.19M flights), summed by airport, date, airline, time of day (tod: "a 5-11am", "b 12-4pm", "c 5pm+") and distance band, joined to that airport-day's storm (wx.*). Measures compare what happened with what the storm justifies: - *_vs_airports: expected = what OTHER airports' flights did for the same airline, the same storm intensity cell (thunder hours x heavy thunder hours x peak gust) and the same time of day. Ratio above 1 = this airport cancels more than comparable storms elsewhere. - *_vs_other_airlines: expected = what OTHER airlines did at the same airport, same storm cell, same time of day. Ratio above 1 = this airline cancels more than its neighbours in the same storm. Flights with no comparable flight elsewhere are left out of the ratios (the *_matched_* columns). Departure delay: late = left 15+ minutes after schedule. Caveats: one summer; weather at the departure airport only (storms en route and at the destination are not matched, though matching on destination storms did not change the airport ranking); association, not cause; small airports' storm days are few, so check storm_ci before quoting a ratio.
source: storm_flights is motherduck.sql("""
  SELECT * FROM ltools.flights.storm_cube
""") extend {
  join_one: wx is storm_days on origin = wx.origin and flight_date = wx.flight_date
  join_one: airport_ci is storm_ci on origin = airport_ci.subject and airport_ci.kind = 'airport cancellations'
  join_one: airline_ci is storm_ci on carrier = airline_ci.subject and airline_ci.kind = 'airline cancellations'
  join_one: airline_late_ci is storm_ci on carrier = airline_late_ci.subject and airline_late_ci.kind = 'airline late departures'

  dimension:
    #" Airline that operated the flight; regional airlines under their own names.
    airline is carrier ?
      pick 'American' when 'AA' pick 'Delta' when 'DL' pick 'United' when 'UA' pick 'Southwest' when 'WN'
      pick 'JetBlue' when 'B6' pick 'Alaska' when 'AS' pick 'Spirit' when 'NK' pick 'Frontier' when 'F9'
      pick 'Allegiant' when 'G4' pick 'SkyWest' when 'OO' pick 'Republic' when 'YX' pick 'Envoy' when 'MQ'
      pick 'PSA' when 'OH' else carrier
    #" Severe or Extreme storm at the departure airport that day.
    is_storm_day is wx.severity_band = '3 Severe' or wx.severity_band = '4 Extreme'
    #" "storm" (Severe/Extreme), "moderate", or "no thunder".
    storm_group is pick 'storm' when is_storm_day pick 'moderate' when wx.severity_band = '2 Moderate' else 'no thunder'
    #" Month name, e.g. "June 2026".
    month_label is wx.month_label
    airport_day is concat(origin, ' ', flight_date::string)

  measure:
    flight_count is n_flights.sum()
    cancellations is n_cancelled.sum()
    # percent
    cancel_rate is n_cancelled.sum() / n_flights.sum()
    #" Share of operated flights that left 15+ minutes late.
    # percent
    late_departure_rate is n_dep_late.sum() / n_operated.sum()
    #" Average departure delay, minutes (operated flights; early = 0).
    avg_departure_delay is dep_delay_min_sum.sum() / n_operated.sum()
    #" Distinct airport-days.
    airport_day_count is count(airport_day)

    #" Cancel rate of the flights that have a comparable flight at another airport.
    # percent
    matched_cancel_rate_vs_airports is n_ap_matched_cancelled.sum() / n_ap_matched.sum()
    #" Cancel rate the storm justifies: other airports, same airline, same storm cell, same time of day.
    # percent
    expected_cancel_rate_vs_airports is exp_cancelled_ap.sum() / n_ap_matched.sum()
    #" Observed / expected cancellations vs other airports (1 = as the storm justifies).
    cancel_ratio_vs_airports is n_ap_matched_cancelled.sum() / exp_cancelled_ap.sum()
    #" Cancellations above (or below, negative) what the storm justifies, vs other airports.
    excess_cancellations_vs_airports is n_ap_matched_cancelled.sum() - exp_cancelled_ap.sum()
    #" Observed / expected late departures vs other airports.
    late_ratio_vs_airports is n_ap_matched_late.sum() / exp_dep_late_ap.sum()

    # percent
    matched_cancel_rate_vs_other_airlines is n_cr_matched_cancelled.sum() / n_cr_matched.sum()
    #" Cancel rate the storm justifies: other airlines at the same airport, same storm cell, same time of day.
    # percent
    expected_cancel_rate_vs_other_airlines is exp_cancelled_cr.sum() / n_cr_matched.sum()
    #" Observed / expected cancellations vs other airlines at the same airport.
    cancel_ratio_vs_other_airlines is n_cr_matched_cancelled.sum() / exp_cancelled_cr.sum()
    excess_cancellations_vs_other_airlines is n_cr_matched_cancelled.sum() - exp_cancelled_cr.sum()
    #" Observed / expected late departures vs other airlines at the same airport.
    late_ratio_vs_other_airlines is n_cr_matched_late.sum() / exp_dep_late_cr.sum()

  #" PLAYBOOK "Does the severity scale mean anything?" Cancel rate, late departures and delay by storm band at the
  #" departure airport. May-July 2026: None 1.0% cancelled, Minor 1.4%, Moderate 2.8%, Severe 6.3%, Extreme 16.9%.
  view: cancellations_by_storm_severity is {
    group_by: severity_band is wx.severity_band
    aggregate: airport_day_count, flight_count, cancellations, cancel_rate, late_departure_rate, avg_departure_delay
    order_by: severity_band
  }

  #" PLAYBOOK "Which airports cancel more than the storm justifies?" Storm days only (Severe/Extreme), airports
  #" ranked by observed / expected cancellations, where expected = other airports' rate for the same airline, the same
  #" storm intensity and the same time of day. ratio_lo/ratio_hi = bootstrap 95% interval; airports with under 5 storm
  #" days are thin. May-July 2026: Newark 2.97x (23.1% cancelled vs 7.8% expected; 1.68-4.21), Washington National
  #" 1.88x, LaGuardia 2.21x (3 storm days), Chicago O'Hare 1.70x (0.93-2.26, not conclusive); Houston IAH 0.11x,
  #" Miami 0.13x, Denver 0.09x, Charlotte 0.31x. Who acts: travel insurers pricing cancellation cover, travel managers
  #" choosing connection hubs, the airports themselves.
  view: airports_cancelling_more_than_the_storm is {
    where: is_storm_day
    group_by: origin
    aggregate:
      storm_days is airport_day_count
      flight_count
      cancel_rate
      expected_cancel_rate_vs_airports
      cancel_ratio_vs_airports
      excess_cancellations_vs_airports
      ratio_lo is airport_ci.lo.max()
      ratio_hi is airport_ci.hi.max()
    order_by: cancel_ratio_vs_airports desc
  }

  #" PLAYBOOK "Which airlines cancel more than the storm justifies?" Storm days only; each airline against the other
  #" airlines at the same airports, same storm cell, same time of day. Airlines with 500+ storm-day flights. May-July
  #" 2026: PSA 1.63x, SkyWest 1.55x, Republic 1.53x cancelled (all intervals above 1); United mainline 0.47x
  #" (0.37-0.55), Delta 0.81x. Southwest: cancels as expected but 1.45x the late departures (it is also late on clear
  #" days, so not storm-specific). Regional airlines' operated flights are LESS often late (Republic 0.83x).
  view: airlines_cancelling_more_than_the_storm is {
    where: is_storm_day
    group_by: airline, carrier_type
    aggregate:
      flight_count
      cancel_rate
      expected_cancel_rate_vs_other_airlines
      cancel_ratio_vs_other_airlines
      excess_cancellations_vs_other_airlines
      ratio_lo is airline_ci.lo.max()
      ratio_hi is airline_ci.hi.max()
      late_departure_rate
      late_ratio_vs_other_airlines
      late_ratio_lo is airline_late_ci.lo.max()
      late_ratio_hi is airline_late_ci.hi.max()
    having: flight_count >= 500
    order_by: cancel_ratio_vs_other_airlines desc
  }

  #" PLAYBOOK "Are short regional hops cut first?" Cancel rate by mainline/regional and flight distance, storm days vs
  #" days without thunder. May-July 2026, storm days: regional under 400 mi 20.8% vs 12.3% for 1,200 mi+; mainline
  #" 7.1% vs 5.9%. Without thunder all are about 1%.
  view: short_regional_hops_cut_first is {
    group_by: carrier_type, dist_band
    aggregate:
      storm_flights is flight_count { where: is_storm_day }
      storm_cancel_rate is cancel_rate { where: is_storm_day }
      no_thunder_cancel_rate is cancel_rate { where: storm_group = 'no thunder' }
    order_by: carrier_type, dist_band
  }

  #" Airport ratio vs other airports by month (storm days), to check a pattern holds month to month.
  view: airport_ratio_by_month is {
    where: is_storm_day
    group_by: origin, month_label
    aggregate: storm_days is airport_day_count, cancel_ratio_vs_airports, cancel_rate, expected_cancel_rate_vs_airports
    order_by: origin, month_label
  }
}

#" Same route, same day: mainline vs regional cancel rates (7,577 rows). comparison = "UA vs SkyWest+Republic", "DL vs SkyWest+Republic", "AA vs Envoy+PSA" (American and its own regionals).
#(agent) Same route, same day: mainline vs regional cancel rates (7,577 rows). comparison = "UA vs SkyWest+Republic", "DL vs SkyWest+Republic", "AA vs Envoy+PSA" (American and its own regionals), "All mainline vs all regional" (also same time of day).
#(agent) One row per comparison x departure airport x date, joined to that day's storm (wx.*). SkyWest and Republic fly for several brands, so a UA or DL row may compare against flights sold by another brand.
source: storm_route_pairs is motherduck.sql("""
  SELECT * FROM ltools.flights.storm_route_pairs
""") extend {
  join_one: wx is storm_days on origin = wx.origin and flight_date = wx.flight_date
  join_one: pair_ci is storm_ci on comparison = pair_ci.subject and pair_ci.kind = 'regional vs mainline, same route-day'
  dimension:
    is_storm_day is wx.severity_band = '3 Severe' or wx.severity_band = '4 Extreme'
    storm_group is pick 'storm' when is_storm_day pick 'moderate' when wx.severity_band = '2 Moderate' else 'no thunder'
  measure:
    # percent
    mainline_cancel_rate is mainline_cancelled.sum() / mainline_flights.sum()
    # percent
    regional_cancel_rate is regional_cancelled.sum() / regional_flights.sum()
    #" Regional cancel rate / mainline cancel rate on the same routes and days.
    regional_to_mainline_ratio is (regional_cancelled.sum() / regional_flights.sum()) / (mainline_cancelled.sum() / mainline_flights.sum())
    #" Regional cancellations above the mainline rate on the same routes and days.
    regional_cancellations_above_mainline is regional_cancelled.sum() - regional_flights.sum() * mainline_cancelled.sum() / mainline_flights.sum()

  #" PLAYBOOK "Do airlines cancel their regional partner first?" Same route, same day. May-July 2026, storm days:
  #" regional 17.1% vs mainline 9.9% (1.73x, 1.48-1.97; 1.36x without storms); United vs SkyWest/Republic 2.06x, Delta
  #" 2.2x; American vs its own Envoy/PSA 1.04x (no gap). Storm-day ratio_lo/ratio_hi = bootstrap 95% interval.
  view: regional_vs_mainline_same_route is {
    group_by: comparison, storm_group
    aggregate:
      mainline_flights_n is mainline_flights.sum()
      mainline_cancel_rate
      regional_flights_n is regional_flights.sum()
      regional_cancel_rate
      regional_to_mainline_ratio
      regional_cancellations_above_mainline
      storm_ratio_lo is pair_ci.lo.max()
      storm_ratio_hi is pair_ci.hi.max()
    order_by: comparison, storm_group
  }
}
